msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:258
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Each signed version gets its own immutable URL (for example /dsa/42/v3). A 'latest' URL may exist; nothing cites it. A new version never overwrites an old one. This fixed-version URL is the one a Consent cites (consent rule 2), and a computable agreement's ODRL policy uid is set to it (P2.3)."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:462
#. Diagram label
msgid "end"
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:471
#. Diagram label
msgid "ended"
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:365
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Existing grants keep citing the old hash. The parties decide, per agreement: they stay valid until expiry, or they are revoked and reissued against the new version (in smart-trust). The old version stays readable."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:104
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Gives each signed version its own immutable URL, records its SHA-256 hash, and writes the DCAT 3 catalogue record. Also sets the catalogue status when the agreement ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../folio-assistant-core/processes/content/dsa-document-lifecycle.bpmn:41
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "How a data sharing agreement is drafted, negotiated, signed, published with a version, a hash and a catalogue record, and later amended, renewed or ended. It covers the agreement as a document folio only: processes P1, P2 and P14 of the DSA process catalogue. Two entry points: a request for an agreement (P1, then P2), and a change to one in force (P14). An amendment or a renewal with a new end date runs P1 and P2 again and publishes a new version linked to the old one; the old version stays readable. Standards named: ODRL 2.2 (the Agreement), DCAT 3 (catalogue record, distributions, checksums, versions) and DCMI Terms (dcterms:source from the policy to the text). No field is defined here. Steps no skill covers yet are user tasks with a no-skill reason starting \"Gap:\". Consent rules, which every agreement drawn here keeps. 1. Legal basis: an agreement carries the odrl:obtainConsent duty only when consent is its legal basis; treatment, public-health law and research waivers do not rest on consent (P1.2a, P1.3). 2. Version pinning: a Consent cites the agreement's fixed-version URL and hash (FHIR R4 Consent.policy.uri), and for a computable agreement the ODRL policy uid is that same URL (P2.1, P2.2b, P2.3); an amendment that widens use needs new consent, one that narrows use does not (P14.3). 3. Plain language: every agreement that needs consent publishes a plain-language summary, hashed with the agreement, and that summary is what the person consents to (P1.2b, P2.2a). How a person gives, withdraws or is asked again for consent is the trust layer's, not this diagram's."
msgstr ""

